/**
@page tutorials_exahype2_euler The Euler Equations

Assuming we have built the ExaHyPE2 engine and the core libraries sucessfully, we can build the Euler project:

    python3 euler.py

Assuming this was succesfull, you should see a number of files have appeared in your working directory.
We will ignore most of these for now, but know that these contain the implementation of the ExaHyPE2 project.
One file in particular is of interest to us now: the file simply named advection is the executable for our implementation.
We will therefore run this executable file by using the following command:

    ./euler-<dimensions>d

The solution folder contains the solution of our problem,
which we will take a look at as soon as we have an understanding of how our problem is defined.

In order to understand what it is that we have executed, let us take a look at the file which defined this project:

    euler.py

As this is a Python script, should surprise noone that we start with the following:

    import peano4
    import exahype2

These merely import the necessary packages from Peano4 and ExaHyPE2
which will let us define and build our project.
Speaking of defining our project, this happens in the next step:

    my_project = exahype2.Project( ["tutorials", "exahype2", "euler"], "EulerTutorial", ".", executable="euler-" + str(dimensions) + "d" )

The parameters here are as follows: first the namespace that the project will have in C++,
then the name of the project itself, then where the project should be generated and finally the name of the executable.
Next we will define and add a solver to this project:

    my_solver = exahype2.solvers.fv.rusanov.GlobalAdaptiveTimeStep(
        name                  = "EulerSolver",
        patch_size            = 3,
        unknowns              = dimensions + 2,
        auxiliary_variables   = 0,
        min_volume_h          = 0.005,
        max_volume_h          = 0.05,
        time_step_relaxation  = 0.5
    )

    my_solver.set_implementation(
        flux                  = exahype2.solvers.fv.PDETerms.User_Defined_Implementation,
        refinement_criterion  = exahype2.solvers.fv.PDETerms.User_Defined_Implementation
    )

Most of this should be self-explanatory and some pieces will be explained in more detail later,
but a few things deserve special mention here.
First is the solver itself, the type exahype2.solvers.fv.rusanov.GlobalAdaptiveTimeStep() indicates the type of solver.
fv stands for finite volumes, this describes the type of the solver, rusanov is the type of flux that is
used to solve the riemann problem at boundaries between cells. Finally global adaptive time step means the solver automatically
chooses an appropriate time step and uses this same time step over the entire domain.

For the parameters of this solver: "name=EulerSolver", this determines the name of the project and therefore of
the files generated by ExaHyPE2 that will define the application. "unknowns=dimensions + 2" and "auxiliary_variables=0" define the
variables of our equation, in this case we have a partial differential equation with 4 (or 5 in 3D) component variables but no auxiliary variables,
which are parameters that can be space-dependent but don't change for the duration of the simulation.
The last thing worth mentioning are the parameters flux and refinement_criterion which we have here set to some user-defined implementation.
We will need to define these in a subsequent step.

Next we define the parameters for our simulation, this is where we define the volume of our domain and the number of dimensions
for example, but also where basic information about the plotting behaviour is defined:

    if dimensions == 2:
        size    = [1.0, 1.0]
        offset  = [0.0, 0.0]
    else:
        size    = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]
        offset  = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]

    my_project.set_global_simulation_parameters(
        dimensions            = dimensions,
        size                  = size,
        offset                = offset,
        min_end_time          = 0.20,
        max_end_time          = 0.20,
        first_plot_time_stamp = 0.0,
        time_in_between_plots = 0.005, # snapshot interval
        periodic_BC           = [False, False, False]
    )

Finally, given all the information above we can generate and build a working application.

    my_project.set_load_balancing( "toolbox::loadbalancing::RecursiveBipartition", "new ::exahype2::LoadBalancingConfiguration()" )
    my_project.set_Peano4_installation( "../../../", mode=peano4.output.CompileMode.Release )
    my_project = my_project.generate_Peano4_project(verbose=True)
    my_project.build(make_clean_first=True, throw_away_data_after_build=True)

Logically, this application is still missing some information about the implementation of this PDE.
We mentioned that we would need to define the flux and the refinement_criterion,
but we have also not yet defined any initial conditions or boundary conditions.
We will do so in the next step.

The astute reader might have noticed that before we built our project,
there were already a few files in the containing folder, most noticeably one that has to do with the project they just built: EulerSolver.cpp.
We will be taking a look at this file next since, as you most likely have already guessed, it contains the implementation of our PDE.

Before we do this however, we will quickly define the PDE that is to come.
The Euler equations describe the movement of a fluid if there is neither loss of
energy due to frictional forces nor heat conduction in the system.
In the finite volume form and given no external forces, the two-dimensional form
of the Euler equations appears as follows:

@f$
    \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left(
    \begin{array}{lr} \rho \\
                      \rho u_1 \\
                      \rho u_2 \\
                      E_t
                      \end{array} \right) +
    \nabla \begin{pmatrix}
                      \rho u_1          & \rho u_2\\
                      \rho u_1^2 + p    & \rho u_1 u_2 \\
                      \rho u_2 u_1      & \rho u_2^2 + p \\
                      (E_t + p) u_1     & (E_t + p) u_2
    \end{pmatrix}  = \vec{0}
@f$

where @f$`\rho`@f$ is the density, @f$`u`@f$ is the velocity, @f$`E_t`@f$ is the energy and @f$`p`@f$ is the pressure.

In order to implement this equation, following things are needed: the initial condition,
the boundary conditions, the maximal eigenvalue, the flux, and the refinement criterion.

We start with the initial conditions: here we define a starting configuration with a uniform
density of 1.0, a uniform starting momentum of 0.0 in both x- and y-directions and a starting energy
that is 1.0 inside of a circle of radius 0.2 around the center of the domain and 1.01 outside of it.
Note that we use momentums and not velocities as our variables of choice,
this will affect the formulation of some of the equations later.

    void tutorials::exahype2::euler::EulerSolver::initialCondition(
    double * __restrict__                         Q, // Q[Dimensions+2+0]
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&   volumeCentre,
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&   volumeH,
    bool                                          gridIsConstructed
    ) {
        Q[0] = 1.0;
        Q[1] = 0.0;
        Q[2] = 0.0;
        #if Dimensions == 3
        Q[3] = 0.0;
        #endif

        #if Dimensions == 2
        tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double> circleCentre = {0.5, 0.5};
        const double distanceFromCenter = tarch::la::norm2(volumeCentre-circleCentre);
        Q[3] = (distanceFromCenter < 0.2 ? 1.0 : 1.01);
        #else
        tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double> circleCentre = {0.5, 0.5, 0.5};
        const double distanceFromCenter = tarch::la::norm2(volumeCentre-circleCentre);
        Q[4] = (distanceFromCenter < 0.2 ? 1.0 : 1.01);
        #endif
    }

Then we can define our boundary conditions.
You can choose any boundary conditions you prefer but we have chosen to use reflecting boundary conditions,
this means the values outside of the boundary are the same as the values inside of the boundary.

    void tutorials::exahype2::euler::EulerSolver::boundaryConditions(
    const double * __restrict__                  Qinside, // Qinside[Dimensions+2+0]
    double * __restrict__                        Qoutside, // Qoutside[Dimensions+2+0]
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&  faceCentre,
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&  volumeH,
    double                                       t,
    int                                          normal
    ) {
        for(int i = 0; i < NumberOfUnknowns+NumberOfAuxiliaryVariables; i++){
            Qoutside[i] = Qinside[i];
        }
    }

Next we can define the eigenvalues of our system. The eigenvalues of the 2-dimensional Euler equations are:

@f$
    \left(
    \begin{array}{lr} \lambda_1 \\
                      \lambda_2 \\
                      \lambda_3
                      \end{array} \right) =
    \left(
    \begin{array}{lr} u - c \\
                      u \\
                      u + c
                      \end{array} \right)
@f$

where @f$`c`@f$ is the wave propagation speed. For dry air, which can be approximated as an ideal gas,
c depends on the pressure as follows:

@f$
    \left(
    \begin{array}{lr} \gamma \\
                      p \\
                      c
                      \end{array} \right) =
    \left(
    \begin{array}{lr}   1.4 \\
                        (\gamma - 1) ( E_t - \frac{1}{2 \rho} (\rho u)^2 ) \\
                        \sqrt[]{\frac{\gamma  p}{\rho}}
                        \end{array} \right)
@f$

    double tutorials::exahype2::euler::EulerSolver::maxEigenvalue(
    const double* __restrict__                    Q, // Q[Dimensions+2+0]
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&   faceCentre,
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&   volumeSize,
    double                                        t,
    double                                        dt,
    int                                           normal
    ) {
        const double irho = 1.0 / Q[0];
        constexpr double gamma = 1.4;

        #if Dimensions == 2
        const double p = (gamma-1) * (Q[3] - 0.5*irho*(Q[1]*Q[1]+Q[2]*Q[2]));
        #else
        const double p = (gamma-1) * (Q[4] - 0.5*irho*(Q[1]*Q[1]+Q[2]*Q[2]+Q[3]*Q[3]));
        #endif

        const double c = std::sqrt(gamma * p * irho);
        const double u = Q[normal+1]*irho;
        return std::max(std::abs(u-c), std::abs(u+c));
    }

And the flux:

    void tutorials::exahype2::euler::EulerSolver::flux(
    const double* __restrict__                    Q, // Q[Dimensions+2+0]
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&   faceCentre,
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&   volumeH,
    double                                        t,
    double                                        dt,
    int                                           normal,
    double* __restrict__                          F // F[Dimensions+2]
    ) {
        const double irho = 1.0 / Q[0];
        constexpr double gamma = 1.4;

        #if Dimensions == 2
        const double p = (gamma-1) * (Q[3] - 0.5*irho*(Q[1]*Q[1]+Q[2]*Q[2]));
        switch (normal) {
            case 0:
                F[0] = Q[1];
                F[1] = Q[1]*Q[1]*irho+p;
                F[2] = Q[1]*Q[2]*irho;
                F[3] = Q[1]*irho*(Q[3]+p);
            break;
            case 1:
                F[0] = Q[2];
                F[1] = Q[1]*Q[2]*irho;
                F[2] = Q[2]*Q[2]*irho+p;
                F[3] = Q[2]*irho*(Q[3]+p);
            break;
        }
        #else
        const double p = (gamma-1) * (Q[4] - 0.5*irho*(Q[1]*Q[1]+Q[2]*Q[2]+Q[3]*Q[3]));
        F[0] = Q[normal+1];
        F[1] = Q[normal+1]*Q[1]*irho;
        F[2] = Q[normal+1]*Q[2]*irho;
        F[3] = Q[normal+1]*Q[3]*irho;
        F[4] = Q[normal+1]*irho*(Q[4]+p);
        F[normal+1] += p;
        #endif
    }

And the refinement criterion:

    ::exahype2::RefinementCommand tutorials::exahype2::euler::EulerSolver::refinementCriterion(
    const double * __restrict__                   Q, // Q[Dimensions+2+0]
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&   volumeX,
    const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions,double>&   volumeH,
    double                                        t
    ) {
        ::exahype2::RefinementCommand result = ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Keep;
        return result;
    }

Finally, we can implement the Euler equations in three dimensions.

The three-dimensional Euler equations are very similar to the two-dimensional equations:

@f$
    \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left(
        \begin{array}{lr}
                    \rho \\
                    \rho u_1 \\
                    \rho u_2 \\
                    \rho u_3 \\
                    E_t 
        \end{array} \right) +
    \nabla
        \begin{pmatrix}
            \rho u_1          & \rho u_2            & \rho u_3\\
            \rho u_1^2 + p    & \rho u_1 u_2        & \rho u_1 u_3 \\
            \rho u_2 u_1      & \rho u_2^2 + p      & \rho u_2 u_3 \\
            \rho u_3 u_1      & \rho u_3 u_2 + p    & \rho u_3^2 + p \\
            (E_t + p) u_1     & (E_t + p) u_2       & (E_t + p) u_3
        \end{pmatrix}  = \vec{0}
@f$

These have the same eigenvalues as the 2D solution:

@f$
    \lambda = \left( \begin{array}{lr}
                     u-c \\
                     u \\
                     u+c \\
        \end{array} \right)
@f$

And you can use the same approximation for the pressure and wave velocity:

@f$
    \left( \begin{array}{lr}
            \gamma \\
            p \\
            c \end{array} \right) =
    \left( \begin{array}{lr}
                     1.4 \\
                     (\gamma - 1) * ( E_t - \frac{\rho \|u\|}{2} ) \\
                    \sqrt[]{\frac{\gamma  p}{\rho}} \\
        \end{array} \right)
@f$

@image html pictures/Euler_3D_start.png
@image html pictures/Euler_3D_end.png
The computed energy of the 3D Euler equations at times t=0.0 and t=1.0

*/
